/**
 * @mainpage MVUE Library
 *
 * @brief Minimum Variance Unbiased Estimator library for sensor fusion.
 *
 * @section introduction Introduction
 *
 * The MVUE library provides lightweight, template-based C++ implementations
 * of Minimum Variance Unbiased Estimators (MVUE) for combining multiple
 * independent measurements of the same quantity.
 *
 * When multiple sensors provide measurements of the same physical quantity
 * with known measurement variances, the measurements can be combined into
 * a single estimate with lower variance than the individual measurements.
 *
 * The library provides estimators for a fixed number of measurements as well
 * as a generic estimator supporting an arbitrary number of measurements.
 *
 *
 * @section mathematical_model Mathematical Model
 *
 * Consider a quantity @f$x@f$ measured by @f$N@f$ independent sensors:
 *
 * @f[
 * x_i = x + v_i
 * @f]
 *
 * where @f$x_i@f$ is the measurement from sensor @f$i@f$ and @f$v_i@f$ is
 * zero-mean measurement noise with variance @f$\sigma_i^2@f$.
 *
 * The minimum variance unbiased estimate is obtained using inverse-variance
 * weighting:
 *
 * @f[
 * \hat{x} =
 * \frac{
 * \displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{x_i}{\sigma_i^2}
 * }{
 * \displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{1}{\sigma_i^2}
 * }
 * @f]
 *
 * The variance of the resulting estimate is:
 *
 * @f[
 * \mathrm{Var}(\hat{x}) =
 * \frac{1}{
 * \displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{1}{\sigma_i^2}
 * }
 * @f]
 *
 * Therefore, measurements with lower variance receive greater weight in the
 * resulting estimate.
 *
 * The inverse-variance weighted estimator is also the Best Linear Unbiased
 * Estimator (BLUE) when the required assumptions are satisfied.
 *
 * In particular, for independent measurements with known variances, the
 * estimator can be written as a linear combination:
 *
 * @f[
 * \hat{x} = \sum_{i=1}^{N} w_i x_i
 * @f]
 *
 * where
 *
 * @f[
 * w_i =
 * \frac{1/\sigma_i^2}
 * {\displaystyle\sum_{j=1}^{N}1/\sigma_j^2}.
 * @f]
 *
 * The weights satisfy the unbiasedness condition:
 *
 * @f[
 * \sum_{i=1}^{N} w_i = 1.
 * @f]
 *
 *
 * @section features Features of Library
 *
 * The library provides:
 *
 * - Two-measurement MVUE estimator.
 * - Three-measurement MVUE estimator.
 * - Four-measurement MVUE estimator.
 * - N-measurement MVUE estimator.
 * - C++ template support for different numeric types.
 * - A lightweight interface suitable for embedded and real-time applications.
 *
 *
  * @section assumptions Assumptions
 *
 * The estimator assumes that:
 *
 * - The measurements estimate the same underlying quantity.
 * - The measurement errors are unbiased.
 * - The measurement variances are known.
 * - The measurement variances are positive.
 * - Measurements are independent when using the inverse-variance weighting
 *   formulation implemented by this library.
 *
 * If measurement errors are correlated, the covariance between measurements
 * must be considered and the simple inverse-variance formulation is no longer
 * sufficient.
 *
 *
 * @section project_structure Project Structure
 *
 * The library is organized as follows:
 *
 * @code{.text}
 * MVUE
 * +-- include
 * |   +-- mvue.h
 * |   +-- mvue_2.h
 * |   +-- mvue_3.h
 * |   +-- mvue_4.h
 * |   `-- mvue_N.h
 * |
 * `-- src
 *     +-- mvue_2.tpp
 *     +-- mvue_3.tpp
 *     +-- mvue_4.tpp
 *     `-- mvue_N.tpp
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section quickstart Quick Start
 *
 * @subsection two_sensor Two-Measurement Example
 *
 * The following example combines two measurements with known variances:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include "mvue_2.h"
 *
 * estimators::mvue::MVUE_2<double> estimator;
 *
 * estimator.init(0.25, 1.0);
 *
 * double estimate = estimator.update(10.2, 9.8);
 * @endcode
 *
 * Here, the first measurement has variance @f$0.25@f$ and the second
 * measurement has variance @f$1.0@f$. Therefore, the first measurement
 * receives a larger weight.
 *
 *
 * @subsection multiple_sensor Multiple Measurements
 *
 * Estimators for three and four measurements are also available:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include "mvue_3.h"
 * #include "mvue_4.h"
 *
 * estimators::mvue::MVUE_3<double> estimator_3;
 * estimators::mvue::MVUE_4<double> estimator_4;
 *
 * estimator_3.init(0.25, 1.0, 0.5);
 * estimator_4.init(0.25, 1.0, 0.5, 2.0);
 *
 * double estimate_3 = estimator_3.update(10.2, 9.8, 10.0);
 * double estimate_4 = estimator_4.update(10.2, 9.8, 10.0, 10.5);
 * @endcode
 *
 *
 * @subsection n_sensor N_Measurement Estimator
 *
 * For an arbitrary number of measurements, use the generic MVUE_N class:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include "mvue_n.h"
 *
 * constexpr std::size_t N = 5;
 *
 * double variances[N] = {
 *     0.25,
 *     1.0,
 *     0.5,
 *     2.0,
 *     0.75
 * };
 *
 * double measurements[N] = {
 *     10.2,
 *     9.8,
 *     10.0,
 *     10.5,
 *     10.1
 * };
 *
 * estimators::mvue::MVUE_N<double, N> estimator;
 *
 * estimator.init(variances);
 *
 * double estimate = estimator.update(measurements);
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section api API Reference
 *
 * For complete documentation of the library API, see **Files/File List** in the content.
 *
 */